# Copy to deploy/.env and fill both values in. Compose reads this file
# automatically when you run `docker compose` from this directory, so the same
# values are used on every boot — which matters more than it looks: see below.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# deploy/.env is gitignored. Keep it; it is not reconstructible.


# The bearer token for the admin/chairman API and the dashboard login. Any
# opaque string — it is compared verbatim, so length and alphabet are yours to
# pick. Generate one with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# If you leave this unset, compose falls back to `dev-admin-token`, which is
# published in every copy of the docs. Set it.
YAAF_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# The key that envelope-encrypts every stored secret (API keys, MCP URLs,
# connector credentials). This one is NOT free-form: it must be a Fernet key,
# 32 url-safe base64 bytes *with* padding. Either of these produces one:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#   python3 -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
#
# What does NOT work is `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)`, the obvious Python idiom
# — it strips the base64 padding, so it looks right and then fails at the
# first secret you store.
#
# Two ways to lose every secret you have stored:
#   - change this value  — the old ciphertext no longer decrypts
#   - leave it unset     — compose substitutes an empty value and the server
#                          refuses to read or write secrets at all
# There is no recovery path for either. Back this up somewhere you trust.
YAAF_MASTER_KEY=
